diff --git a/docs/guide/coverage/os/alpaquita.md b/docs/guide/coverage/os/alpaquita.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..d6f2deb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/guide/coverage/os/alpaquita.md
@@ -0,0 +1,63 @@
+# BellSoft Alpaquita Linux
+Trivy supports these scanners for OS packages.
+
+|    Scanner    | Supported |
+| :-----------: | :-------: |
+|     SBOM      |     ✓     |
+| Vulnerability |     ✓     |
+|    License    |     ✓     |
+
+The table below outlines the features offered by Trivy.
+
+|               Feature                | Supported |
+|:------------------------------------:|:---------:|
+|    Detect unfixed vulnerabilities    |     ✓     |
+| [Dependency graph][dependency-graph] |     ✓     |
+|        End of life awareness         |     ✓     |
+
+## SBOM
+Alpaquita Linux uses apk, like [Alpine Linux](alpine.md#sbom).
+
+Alpaquita ships apk-tools 3, which keeps its database at `/var/lib/apk/db/installed`
+rather than `/lib/apk/db/installed`. Trivy looks in both locations.
+
+## Vulnerability
+BellSoft publishes its own security advisories in OSV format, and these are used
+when scanning Alpaquita for vulnerabilities.
+
+Advisories are scoped to a release, taken from `VERSION_ID` in `/etc/os-release`:
+`stream` for the rolling release and `23` or `25` for the LTS branches. The libc
+flavor (`musl` or `glibc`) does not affect advisory matching.
+
+Advisories are keyed on the name of the source package. When a package is
+published under a name other than the one it is built from, Trivy resolves the
+advisory through the apk `provides` field. This is what connects, for example, the
+installed package `liberica21-lite-jdk-no-deps` (built from `liberica21-lite`) to
+the advisories published for `openjdk21-lite`.
+
+Unfixed vulnerabilities are reported: an advisory that records when a
+vulnerability was introduced but not when it was fixed applies to every version
+from that point on.
+
+### End of life awareness
+`stream` is a rolling release and never reaches end of life. LTS releases are
+supported for at least four years; Trivy reports a release as EOSL once its end of
+commercial support has passed.
+
+### Data Source
+See [here](../../scanner/vulnerability.md#data-sources).
+
+## License
+Same as [Alpine Linux](alpine.md#license).
+
+# BellSoft Hardened Containers
+BellSoft Hardened Containers are built on Alpaquita Linux and are identified
+separately by `ID=bellsoft-hardened-containers` in `/etc/os-release`. They are
+scanned exactly like Alpaquita, against the advisories BellSoft publishes for the
+hardened package set.
+
+The distroless variants ship no shell and no `apk` binary, but they do keep the
+apk database at `/var/lib/apk/db/installed`, so package detection works without
+any additional configuration.
+
+[dependency-graph]: ../../configuration/reporting.md#show-origins-of-vulnerable-dependencies
